# =============================================================================
#  Build context exclusions.
# =============================================================================
#  Everything here is either rebuilt inside the image, host-specific, or a
#  secret. Excluding it keeps the context small (faster builds) and — for the
#  .env rules — makes it *impossible* to accidentally COPY credentials into a
#  layer, which is the one Docker mistake you cannot undo after `docker push`.
# =============================================================================

# Reinstalled in the image. Host node_modules may hold darwin/arm64 binaries
# that cannot execute on the linux image — copying them in is a silent breakage.
node_modules

# A previous host build. Stale output would be shadowed by the in-image build
# anyway, but copying it wastes context and can confuse layer caching.
.next
out
build

#  NEVER let these into an image. `.env` holds the admin password, the JWT
# secret and the Partner API token.
.env
.env.*

# ── Credential files on disk ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The same block backend/.dockerignore carries, and it is here for a reason that
# is easy to miss: .gitignore already excludes these, but .gitignore does NOT
# govern a Docker build context. `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile below reads the
# working directory, not the index, so a key file sitting untracked beside
# package.json would be swept into a layer — and `docker history` and
# `docker save` both read a layer back out.
#
# Nothing in the dashboard reads a key today. The rule is here so that adding
# one later is not silently also a disclosure.
secret/
secrets/
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*-key.json
*service-account*.json
*credentials*.json

# VCS history and local tooling noise.
.git
.gitignore
.vercel
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store

# Docs are not needed at build or run time.
*.md

# Logs and coverage.
coverage
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
